# /backend/.env

# AI Model Provider
GOOGLE_API_KEY="<your-gemini-api-key>"
# OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# OpenRouter — only needed if you set one of the *_FALLBACK_MODEL vars below. Get a key
# at https://openrouter.ai/keys.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY="<your-openrouter-api-key>"

# Model identifiers — required, no in-code default. Set these to actual Gemini model
# ids you have access to before running; the app fails fast at startup if unset.
PLANNER_MODEL="<set-your-gemini-model-id>"
WORKER_MODEL="<set-your-gemini-model-id>"
# Falls back to WORKER_MODEL if unset (core/llm.py's ModelRole.VERDICT resolution) —
# set separately to give verdict_node its own quota bucket from the worker's hot loop.
VERDICT_MODEL="<set-your-gemini-model-id>"
MEMORY_EXTRACTION_MODEL="<set-your-gemini-model-id>"

# Optional — an OpenRouter model id (e.g. "openai/gpt-oss-20b:free") that PLANNER/WORKER/
# VERDICT fall back to on a Gemini rate-limit/server error (core/llm.py's with_fallback());
# requires OPENROUTER_API_KEY above. Leave unset for no fallback (the default). No
# MEMORY_FALLBACK_MODEL — langmem wires MEMORY_EXTRACTION_MODEL internally, which
# with_fallback() can't wrap.
# PLANNER_FALLBACK_MODEL="<openrouter-model-id>"
# WORKER_FALLBACK_MODEL="<openrouter-model-id>"
# VERDICT_FALLBACK_MODEL="<openrouter-model-id>"
MEMORY_EMBEDDING_MODEL="<set-your-gemini-embedding-model-id>"
# Must match MEMORY_EMBEDDING_MODEL's output width — e.g. Gemini's text-embedding-004
# outputs 768 dims; gemini-embedding-001 supports configurable output up to 3072.
MEMORY_EMBEDDING_DIMS="<set-to-match-MEMORY_EMBEDDING_MODEL's-output-width>"

# Semantic scenario dedup (core/memory.py's drop_semantic_duplicates) — reuses the SAME
# embedding model/dims above. Cosine-similarity floor for treating two test case goals
# as "the same behavior under test, worded differently." Deliberately high: a
# false-positive here silently drops real coverage, which is worse than an occasional
# near-duplicate slipping through (the cheap, zero-risk exact-match check in
# core/run_planning.py already catches literal repeats for free).
SCENARIO_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD=0.93

# API Configuration
PORT=8000
HOST="0.0.0.0"
# floor(RPM_limit / requests_per_worker_per_minute) for WORKER_MODEL — see backend/.env
# for the worked example this default came from. Re-derive from your own AI Studio
# rate-limit page and summary.llm.requests (Stage 0.5) rather than trusting this as-is.
MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS=4
SSE_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=1.0

# Recon (backend/nodes/recon/, graph/builder.py's route_to_recon) — interactive,
# per-Feature exploration that clicks into a flow before the worker fan-out, grounding
# scenario generation in what the app actually offers instead of the static checklist in
# TEST_CASE_AUTHORING_GUIDELINES. Defaults to false — an explicit opt-in, since it
# interacts with and may submit non-destructive forms on the real target site.
RECON_ENABLED=false
RECON_MAX_TURNS=12
SCENARIOS_PER_FEATURE_MAX=6
SCENARIOS_PER_RUN_MAX=18

# LLM call bounding (backend/core/llm.py) — applies to every Gemini role (planner,
# worker, verdict, memory). LLM_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS bounds a single call that hangs
# rather than erroring (max_retries only reacts to a COMPLETED 429/5xx response).
# LLM_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND/LLM_MAX_BURST configure a shared token-bucket rate limiter
# EVERY role's model call awaits before sending — the actual fix for concurrent worker
# branches all issuing their first call in the same second: it makes a 429 from our own
# request pattern structurally unreachable instead of something to detect and back off
# from after the fact. Derive requests_per_second from your account's real RPM (same
# number MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS above was derived from) — e.g. RPM=15 -> 0.25/s.
LLM_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
LLM_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND=0.25
LLM_MAX_BURST=5

# Adaptive worker execution (backend/nodes/worker/nodes.py, backend/nodes/agent_loop.py).
# A test case starts with MAX_TOOL_TURNS turns and earns TURN_BUDGET_BONUS more, up to
# MAX_TOOL_TURNS_CEILING, each time it actually handles a real deviation from the
# written steps or gets an ask_human answer — so the cost of adapting only rises for the
# case that needed it, instead of raising the flat turn cap (and worst-case quota burn)
# for every case whether it deviates or not.
MAX_TOOL_TURNS=8
MAX_TOOL_TURNS_CEILING=20
TURN_BUDGET_BONUS=3
# How many times in a row the exact same tool call (name + args) can repeat before
# tool_node nudges the model to try something different or call ask_human.
STUCK_REPEAT_THRESHOLD=3
# Cap on how many core/run_knowledge.py facts (deviations recorded by OTHER test cases
# in this same run) get folded into a fresh worker's outbound context per turn.
RUN_KNOWLEDGE_MAX_FACTS=8

# Dead-task bounds (backend/mcp/client.py, backend/nodes/agent_loop.py,
# backend/nodes/worker/session.py). Three independent layers, since nothing previously
# bounded how long a stalled browser/page could hold a worker slot: TOOL_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# bounds a SINGLE MCP tool call (browser_click, browser_navigate, ...);
# SESSION_OPEN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS bounds spawning the Playwright MCP subprocess and
# completing its handshake; SCENARIO_DEADLINE_SECONDS is a coarse per-test-case
# wall-clock backstop (checked once per agent_node turn) for a case that times out on
# nearly every tool call without ever converging — MAX_TOOL_TURNS_CEILING above bounds
# turn COUNT, not turns-times-worst-case-latency. A human-in-the-loop pause
# (ask_human / risky-action review) pushes the deadline forward on resume rather than
# counting against it, so a slow human answer never trips it.
TOOL_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90
SESSION_OPEN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
SCENARIO_DEADLINE_SECONDS=600

# Chat-first discovery planning (backend/nodes/discovery.py, backend/nodes/planner_explore.py)
# — the shallow site crawl run before proposing a test plan, and the bounds on the
# discovery conversation itself. Discovery reuses PLANNER_MODEL rather than a separate
# required model var, since the chat conversation IS the planner for a chat-approved run.
PLANNER_CRAWL_MAX_PAGES=8
PLANNER_CRAWL_MAX_DEPTH=2
SITE_MAP_TTL_HOURS=24
MAX_DISCOVERY_TURNS=20
MAX_EXTRA_DIVES=5

# History dashboard (backend/core/history.py) — session-history metadata store backing
# GET /history and /history/stats. Its own dedicated connection pool, separate from the
# checkpointer's single-connection bottleneck, since history writes on every run's status
# transition must never contend with checkpoint I/O.
HISTORY_POOL_MAX_SIZE=5

# Postgres — checkpointing (fault tolerance / HITL pause-resume) and long-term memory
# (pgvector) share this same database, just different tables. host/port/user below
# match a default local Postgres install (localhost:5432, user "postgres") — fill in
# <db-password> and <db-name> for your instance. If pointing this at a database
# already named with mixed case (e.g. "OmniTest" in pgAdmin), use that exact case
# here — Postgres treats a quoted/pgAdmin-created name as case-sensitive. Requires
# the pgvector extension enabled on it: `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;`
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:<db-password>@localhost:5432/<db-name>"

# Connection pool sizes (backend/graph/checkpointer.py, backend/core/memory.py) — both
# used to open a SINGLE connection despite their own docstrings claiming a pool, which
# serialized every checkpoint write (one per worker tool call, across
# MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS parallel branches) and every SSE client's once-a-second state
# read onto one round trip at a time. The checkpointer takes the heavier, bursty load;
# the memory store is touched only ~2x per run.
CHECKPOINTER_POOL_MAX_SIZE=10
MEMORY_POOL_MAX_SIZE=5

# LangSmith Tracing (Highly recommended for debugging LangGraph)
LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2="true"
LANGCHAIN_API_KEY="ls__..."
LANGCHAIN_PROJECT="OmniTest-Dev"

# Playwright MCP server (defaults shown below are used if unset). --isolated is
# required for our concurrent-worker design, not optional; --headless keeps every
# automated browser invisible to whoever is running this (it otherwise pops a real,
# visible window on the desktop by default). Devtools (trace/video capture) and storage
# (Stage 3 shared login, nodes/auth/nodes.py) capabilities are NOT set here — mcp/client.py
# appends --config=<bundled playwright-mcp-config.json> itself, since the installed
# @playwright/mcp can't combine multiple --caps values in one CLI arg (confirmed bug,
# see mcp/client.py). Add other flags here if needed, just don't put --caps back on
# this line.
PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_COMMAND=npx
PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_ARGS=-y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --headless

# Per-mutation video clips (backend/nodes/worker/evidence.py). Video is NOT recorded
# for the whole test case — only a short clip around each real page mutation
# (browser_click, browser_type, browser_navigate, ...), padded this many seconds
# before/after. A session-length recording used to capture every turn spent waiting on
# an LLM decision between actions too, which dominated its length and showed nothing —
# a real run produced a 4-5 minute video where only the last few seconds had anything
# happening on screen. This is pure wall-clock cost (no LLM/API quota spent): each
# mutating action in a test case adds roughly PRE + action-time + POST to that test
# case's real duration, so lower these if a plan has many mutations per case and total
# run time matters more than per-clip context.
ACTION_CLIP_PRE_SECONDS=3
ACTION_CLIP_POST_SECONDS=3

# Idle-session backstop (backend/nodes/worker/session.py). A worker's Playwright
# session deliberately survives a human-in-the-loop pause (ask_human / risky-action
# review) so the resume continues against the SAME browser instead of a blank one —
# api.py's _drive() only sweeps sessions when a run actually finishes, not when it
# merely pauses. This TTL is the backstop for the one case that leaves open: a pause
# that's never resumed (the reviewer closes the tab, walks away). A session idle past
# this many seconds, and not belonging to a run currently executing a node, is closed.
SESSION_IDLE_TTL_SECONDS=1800
SESSION_REAP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
